# Pausing work before the usage limit

Madhukar switches Claude accounts when one nears its limit. Work that keeps spending while they are away must
pause BEFORE the limit, not fail at it. Goal: every such job pauses at 90% of any window (5-hour, 7-day,
per-model), or at 80% when usage rose 20 points in the last 15 minutes, and nothing is killed.

## Steps

1. **Read usage from Claude Code itself.** It takes about 2 seconds and spends no tokens:
   `echo '{"type":"control_request","request_id":"u","request":{"subtype":"get_usage"}}' | claude -p --input-format stream-json --output-format stream-json --verbose`
   Read `rate_limits` (5-hour, 7-day, per-model `utilization`, in percent) from the `control_response` line.
   The status line and claude-hud snapshots lag: they update only while a session is active (2026-10-06: the
   status line read 83% while real usage reached 100%, so the pause never fired).
   Done when you have a percent for every window.
2. **Before starting the work, start a watcher** that pauses it at the threshold above. Pausing has two parts:
   a pause file that the work checks before each model call (stops new steps), then `SIGSTOP` to the running
   processes (a step already talking to the model finishes, and nothing is killed). Then notify Madhukar.
{{- if .work }}
   Example to copy: `claude-code-plugins` repo, `plugins/engineering/skills/trd/evals/usage_guard.py`
   (`watch --at 90`, `resume`, `status`).
{{- end }}
   Done when the watcher is running and you have told Madhukar how to check it.
3. **Count only running time against time limits.** A wall-clock step timeout fires on resume and kills work that
   had finished (2026-10-06: a two-day pause crashed 14 eval batches on resume). Either time limits count running
   time only, or the pause stops processes only between steps.
4. **Before raising parallelism, read usage again.** Many parallel runs use a 5-hour window in minutes (8 parallel
   Opus eval runs went from 49% to 100% in about 12 minutes).
5. **Resume only when Madhukar says they switched accounts.** Remove the pause file and send `SIGCONT`.
   A step that failed on a usage-limit error produced no result: mark it and re-run it.
   Done when no step is marked failed-on-limit.
